## an3.48
an3.48:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, great sal trees grow in three ways supported by the Himalayas, the king of mountains. | T: “Bhikkhus, based on the Himalayas, the king of mountains, great sal trees grow in three ways.
an3.48:1.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.48:1.3 | S: The branches, leaves, and foliage; the bark and shoots; and the softwood and heartwood. | T: (1) They grow in branches, leaves, and foliage; (2) they grow in bark and shoots; and (3) they grow in softwood and heartwood.
an3.48:1.4 | S: Great sal trees grow in these three ways supported by the Himalayas, the king of mountains. | T: Based on the Himalayas, the king of mountains, great sal trees grow in these three ways.
an3.48:2.1 | S: In the same way, a family grows in three ways supported by a family head with faith. | T: “So too, when the head of a family is endowed with faith, the people in the family who depend on him grow in three ways.
an3.48:2.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.48:2.3 | S: Faith, ethics, and wisdom. | T: (1) They grow in faith; (2) they grow in virtuous behavior; and (3) they grow in wisdom.
an3.48:2.4 | S: A family grows in these three ways supported by a family head with faith. | T: When the head of a family is endowed with faith, the people in the family who depend on him grow in these three ways.”
an3.48:3.1 | S: Supported by the rocky mountain | T: Just as the trees that grow in dependence on a rocky mountain
an3.48:3.2 | S: in the wilds, the formidable forest, | T: in a vast forest wilderness
an3.48:3.3 | S: the tree grows | T: (none)
an3.48:3.4 | S: to become lord of the forest. | T: might become great “woodland lords,”
an3.48:4.1 | S: So too, when the family head | T: so, when the head of a family here
an3.48:4.2 | S: is ethical and faithful, | T: possesses faith and virtue,
an3.48:4.3 | S: supported by them, they grow: | T: (none)
an3.48:4.4 | S: children, partners, and kin, | T: his wife, children, and relatives all grow in dependence upon him;
an3.48:4.5 | S: colleagues, relatives, | T: so too his friends, his family circle,
an3.48:4.6 | S: and those dependent for their livelihood. | T: and those dependent on him.
an3.48:5.1 | S: Seeing the ethical conduct of the virtuous, | T: Those possessed of discernment, seeing that virtuous man’s good conduct,
an3.48:5.2 | S: the generosity and good deeds, | T: his generosity and good deeds,
an3.48:5.3 | S: those who see clearly | T: (none)
an3.48:5.4 | S: do likewise. | T: emulate his example.
an3.48:6.1 | S: Having practiced the teaching here, | T: Having lived here in accord with Dhamma,
an3.48:6.2 | S: the path that goes to a good place, | T: the path leading to a good destination,
an3.48:6.3 | S: they delight in the heavenly realm, | T: those who desire sensual pleasures rejoice,
an3.48:6.4 | S: enjoying all the pleasures they desire.” | T: delighting in the deva world.
an3.48:6.5 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.28
an8.28:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Sāriputta went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him: | T: Then the Venerable Sāriputta approached the Blessed One…. The Blessed One then said to him:
an8.28:1.2 | S: “Sāriputta, how many powers does a mendicant who has ended the defilements have that qualify them to claim: | T: “Sāriputta, when a bhikkhu’s taints have been destroyed, how many powers does he possess by reason of which he can claim:
an8.28:1.3 | S: ‘My defilements have ended’?” | T: ‘My taints have been destroyed’?”
an8.28:1.4 | S: “Sir, a mendicant who has ended the defilements has eight powers that qualify them to claim: | T: “Bhante, when a bhikkhu’s taints have been destroyed, he possesses eight powers by reason of which he can claim:
an8.28:1.5 | S: ‘My defilements have ended.’ | T: ‘My taints have been destroyed.’
an8.28:2.1 | S: What eight? | T: What eight?
an8.28:2.2 | S: Firstly, a mendicant with defilements ended has clearly seen with right wisdom all conditions as truly impermanent. | T: (1) “Here, Bhante, a bhikkhu with taints destroyed has clearly seen all conditioned phenomena as they really are with correct wisdom as impermanent.
an8.28:2.3 | S: This is a power that a mendicant who has ended the defilements relies on to claim: | T: This is a power of a bhikkhu with taints destroyed on the basis of which he can claim:
an8.28:2.4 | S: ‘My defilements have ended.’ | T: ‘My taints have been destroyed.’
an8.28:3.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant with defilements ended has clearly seen with right wisdom that sensual pleasures are truly like a pit of glowing coals. | T: (2) “Again, a bhikkhu with taints destroyed has clearly seen sensual pleasures as they really are with correct wisdom as similar to a charcoal pit.
an8.28:3.2 | S: This is a power that a mendicant who has ended the defilements relies on to claim: | T: This is a power of a bhikkhu with taints destroyed….
an8.28:3.3 | S: ‘My defilements have ended.’ | T: (none)
an8.28:4.1 | S: Furthermore, the mind of a mendicant with defilements ended slants, slopes, and inclines to seclusion. They’re withdrawn, loving renunciation, and have totally eliminated defiling influences. | T: (3) “Again, the mind of a bhikkhu with taints destroyed slants, slopes, and inclines to seclusion; it is withdrawn, delighting in renunciation, and is entirely finished with all things that are a basis for the taints.
an8.28:4.2 | S: This is a power that a mendicant who has ended the defilements relies on to claim: | T: This is a power of a bhikkhu with taints destroyed….
an8.28:4.3 | S: ‘My defilements have ended.’ | T: (none)
an8.28:5.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant with defilements ended has well developed the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: (4) “Again, a bhikkhu with taints destroyed has developed and well developed the four establishments of mindfulness.
an8.28:5.2 | S: This is a power that a mendicant who has ended the defilements relies on to claim: | T: Since that is so, this is a power of a bhikkhu with taints destroyed….
an8.28:5.3 | S: ‘My defilements have ended.’ | T: (none)
an8.28:6.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant with defilements ended has well developed the four bases of psychic power … | T: (5)–(8) “Again, a bhikkhu with taints destroyed has developed and well developed the four bases for psychic potency …
an8.28:6.2 | S: the five faculties … | T: the five spiritual faculties …
an8.28:6.3 | S: the seven awakening factors … | T: the seven factors of enlightenment …
an8.28:6.4 | S: the noble eightfold path. | T: the noble eightfold path.
an8.28:6.5 | S: This is a power that a mendicant who has ended the defilements relies on to claim: | T: This is a power of a bhikkhu with taints destroyed on the basis of which he can claim:
an8.28:6.6 | S: ‘My defilements have ended.’ | T: ‘My taints have been destroyed.’
an8.28:7.1 | S: A mendicant who has ended the defilements possesses these eight powers that qualify them to claim: | T: “Bhante, when a bhikkhu’s taints have been destroyed, he possesses these eight powers on the basis of which he can claim:
an8.28:7.2 | S: ‘My defilements have ended.’” | T: ‘My taints have been destroyed.’”
an8.28:7.3 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn5.4
sn5.4:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn5.4:1.2 | S: Then the nun Vijayā robed up in the morning … | T: Then, in the morning, the bhikkhuni Vijaya dressed …
sn5.4:1.3 | S: and sat at the root of a tree for the day’s meditation. | T: she sat down at the foot of a tree for the day’s abiding.
sn5.4:1.4 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, wanting to make the nun Vijayā feel fear, terror, and goosebumps, wanting to make her fall away from immersion, went up to her and addressed her in verse: | T: Then Mara the Evil One, desiring to arouse fear, trepidation, and terror in the bhikkhuni Vijaya, desiring to make her fall away from concentration, approached her and addressed her in verse:
sn5.4:2.1 | S: “You’re so young and beautiful, | T: “You are so young and beautiful,
sn5.4:2.2 | S: and I’m a youth in my prime. | T: And I too am a youth in my prime.
sn5.4:2.3 | S: Come, my lady, let us enjoy | T: Come, noble lady, let us rejoice
sn5.4:2.4 | S: the music of a five-piece band.” | T: With the music of a fivefold ensemble.”
sn5.4:3.1 | S: Then the nun Vijayā thought, | T: Then it occurred to the bhikkhuni Vijaya:
sn5.4:3.2 | S: “Who’s speaking this verse, a human or a non-human?” | T: “Now who is this…?
sn5.4:3.3 | S: Then she thought, | T: (none)
sn5.4:3.4 | S: “This is Māra the Wicked, wanting to make me feel fear, terror, and goosebumps, wanting to make me fall away from immersion!” | T: This is Mara the Evil One … desiring to make me fall away from concentration.”
sn5.4:3.5 | S: Then Vijayā, knowing that this was Māra the Wicked, replied to him in verse: | T: Then the bhikkhuni Vijaya, having understood, “This is Mara the Evil One,” replied to him in verses:
sn5.4:4.1 | S: “Sights, sounds, tastes, smells, | T: “Forms, sounds, tastes, odours,
sn5.4:4.2 | S: and touches so delightful. | T: And delightful tactile objects—
sn5.4:4.3 | S: I hand them right back to you, Māra, | T: I offer them right back to you,
sn5.4:4.4 | S: for I have no use for them. | T: For I, O Mara, do not need them.
sn5.4:5.1 | S: This body is rotting, | T: “I am repelled and humiliated By this foul, putrid body,
sn5.4:5.2 | S: decaying and frail. | T: Subject to break up, fragile:
sn5.4:5.3 | S: I’m horrified and repelled by it, | T: (none)
sn5.4:5.4 | S: and I’ve eradicated sensual craving. | T: I’ve uprooted sensual craving.
sn5.4:6.1 | S: There are beings in the realm of luminous form, | T: “As to those beings who fare amidst form,
sn5.4:6.2 | S: and others stuck in the formless. | T: And those who abide in the formless,
sn5.4:6.3 | S: I’ve destroyed the darkness | T: (none)
sn5.4:6.4 | S: regarding all such peaceful attainments.” | T: And those peaceful attainments too: Everywhere darkness has been destroyed.”
sn5.4:7.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The nun Vijayā knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Mara the Evil One, realizing “The bhikkhuni Vijaya knows me,” sad and disappointed, disappeared right there.

## an3.37
an3.37:1.1 | S: “On the eighth day of the fortnight, mendicants, the ministers and councillors of the four great kings wander about the world, thinking: | T: “Bhikkhus, (1) on the eighth of the fortnight, the ministers and assembly members of the four great kings wander over this world, thinking:
an3.37:1.2 | S: ‘Hopefully most humans are paying due respect to their parents, ascetics and brahmins, honoring the elders in their families, observing and keeping vigil on the sabbath, and making merit.’ | T: ‘We hope there are many people who behave properly toward their mother and father, behave properly toward ascetics and brahmins, honor the elders of the family, observe the uposatha, keep the extra observance days, and do meritorious deeds.’
an3.37:1.3 | S: And on the fourteenth day of the fortnight, the sons of the four great kings wander about the world, thinking: | T: (2) On the fourteenth of the fortnight, the sons of the four great kings wander over this world, thinking:
an3.37:1.4 | S: ‘Hopefully most humans are paying due respect to their parents … and making merit.’ | T: ‘We hope there are many people who behave properly toward their mother and father … … and do meritorious deeds.’
an3.37:1.5 | S: And on the fifteenth day sabbath, the four great kings themselves wander about the world, thinking: | T: (3) On the fifteenth, the uposatha day, the four great kings themselves wander over this world, thinking:
an3.37:1.6 | S: ‘Hopefully most humans are paying due respect to their parents … and making merit.’ | T: ‘We hope there are many people who behave properly toward their mother and father … and do meritorious deeds.’
an3.37:2.1 | S: If only a few humans are paying due respect to their parents … and making merit, | T: “If, bhikkhus, there are few people who behave properly toward their mother and father … and do meritorious deeds,
an3.37:2.2 | S: the four great kings address the gods of the thirty-three, seated together in the Hall of Clear Right: | T: the four great kings report this to the Tāvatiṁsa devas when they meet and are sitting together in the Sudhamma council hall:
an3.37:2.3 | S: ‘Only a few humans are paying due respect to their parents … and making merit.’ | T: ‘Revered sirs, there are few people who behave properly toward their mother and father … and do meritorious deeds.’
an3.37:2.4 | S: Then the gods of the thirty-three are disappointed, thinking, | T: Then, because of this, the Tāvatiṁsa devas become displeased, saying:
an3.37:2.5 | S: ‘The heavenly hosts will dwindle, while the titan hosts will swell!’ | T: ‘Alas, the celestial company will decline and the company of asuras will flourish!’
an3.37:3.1 | S: But if many humans are paying due respect to their parents … and making merit, | T: “But if there are many people who behave properly toward their mother and father … and do meritorious deeds,
an3.37:3.2 | S: the four great kings address the gods of the thirty-three, seated together in the Hall of Clear Right: | T: the four great kings report this to the Tāvatiṁsa devas when they meet and are sitting together in the Sudhamma council hall:
an3.37:3.3 | S: ‘Many humans are paying due respect to their parents … and making merit.’ | T: ‘Revered sirs, there are many people who behave properly toward their mother and father, behave properly toward ascetics and brahmins, honor the elders of the family, observe the uposatha, keep the extra observance days, and do meritorious deeds.’
an3.37:3.4 | S: Then the gods of the thirty-three are pleased, thinking, | T: Then, because of this, the Tāvatiṁsa devas become elated, saying:
an3.37:3.5 | S: ‘The heavenly hosts will swell, while the titan hosts will dwindle!’ | T: ‘Indeed, the celestial company will flourish and the company of asuras will decline!’”
an3.37:4.1 | S: Once upon a time, Sakka, lord of gods, guiding the gods of the thirty-three, spoke this verse: | T: “Bhikkhus, once in the past, when Sakka, ruler of the devas, was guiding the Tāvatiṁsa devas, he recited this verse:
an3.37:5.1 | S: ‘Whoever wants to be like me | T: “‘The person who would be like me
an3.37:5.2 | S: would observe the sabbath | T: should observe the uposatha
an3.37:5.3 | S: complete in all eight factors, | T: complete in the eight factors,
an3.37:5.4 | S: on the fourteenth and the fifteenth days, | T: on the fourteenth, fifteenth,
an3.37:5.5 | S: and the eighth day of the fortnight, | T: and eighth of the fortnight,
an3.37:5.6 | S: as well as on the fortnightly special displays.’ | T: and during special fortnights.’
an3.37:6.1 | S: But that verse was poorly sung by Sakka, lord of gods, not well sung; poorly spoken, not well spoken. | T: “This verse, bhikkhus, was badly recited by Sakka, ruler of the devas, not well recited. It was badly stated, not well stated.
an3.37:6.2 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
an3.37:6.3 | S: Because Sakka, lord of gods, is not free of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: Because Sakka, ruler of the devas, is not devoid of lust, hatred, and delusion.
an3.37:7.1 | S: But for a mendicant who is perfected—with defilements ended, who has completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and is rightly freed through enlightenment—it is appropriate to say: | T: But in the case of a bhikkhu who is an arahant—one whose taints are destroyed, who has lived the spiritual life, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, reached his own goal, utterly destroyed the fetters of existence, one completely liberated through final knowledge—it is fitting for him to say:
an3.37:8.1 | S: ‘Whoever wants to be like me | T: “‘The person who would be like me …
an3.37:8.2 | S: would observe the sabbath, | T: (none)
an3.37:8.3 | S: complete in all eight factors, | T: (none)
an3.37:8.4 | S: on the fourteenth and the fifteenth days, | T: (none)
an3.37:8.5 | S: and the eighth day of the fortnight, | T: (none)
an3.37:8.6 | S: as well as on the fortnightly special displays.’ | T: and during special fortnights.’
an3.37:9.1 | S: Why is that? | T: “For what reason?
an3.37:9.2 | S: Because that mendicant is free of greed, hate, and delusion.” | T: Because that bhikkhu is devoid of lust, hatred, and delusion.”
an3.37:9.3 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.47
an3.47:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, the conditioned has these three characteristics. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these three characteristics that define the conditioned.
an3.47:1.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.47:1.3 | S: Arising is evident, vanishing is evident, and change while persisting is evident. | T: An arising is seen, a vanishing is seen, and its alteration while it persists is seen.
an3.47:1.4 | S: These are the three characteristics of the conditioned.” | T: These are the three characteristics that define the conditioned.
an3.47:1.5 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)
an3.47:2.0 | S: Characteristics of the Unconditioned | T: (none)
an3.47:2.1 | S: “The unconditioned has these three characteristics. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these three characteristics that define the unconditioned.
an3.47:2.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.47:2.3 | S: No arising is evident, no vanishing is evident, and no change while persisting is evident. | T: No arising is seen, no vanishing is seen, and no alteration while it persists is seen.
an3.47:2.4 | S: These are the three characteristics of the unconditioned.” | T: These are the three characteristics that define the unconditioned.”
an3.47:2.5 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.42
an5.42:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a true person is born in a family for the benefit, welfare, and happiness of the people. | T: “Bhikkhus, when a good person is born in a family, it is for the good, welfare, and happiness of many people.
an5.42:1.2 | S: For the benefit, welfare, and happiness of mother and father; | T: It is for the good, welfare, and happiness of (1) his mother and father,
an5.42:1.3 | S: children and partners; | T: (2) his wife and children,
an5.42:1.4 | S: bondservants, workers, and staff; | T: (3) his slaves, workers, and servants,
an5.42:1.5 | S: friends and colleagues; | T: (4) his friends and companions,
an5.42:1.6 | S: and ascetics and brahmins. | T: and (5) ascetics and brahmins.
an5.42:2.1 | S: It’s like a great rain cloud, which nourishes all the crops for the benefit, welfare, and happiness of the people. | T: Just as a great rain cloud, nurturing all the crops, appears for the good, welfare, and happiness of many people,
an5.42:2.2 | S: In the same way, a true person is born in a family for the benefit, welfare, and happiness of the people. … | T: so too, when a good person is born in a family, it is for the good, welfare, and happiness of many people.
an5.42:2.3 | P: mātāpitūnaṁ atthāya hitāya sukhāya hoti; | T: It is for the good, welfare, and happiness of his mother and father …
an5.42:2.4 | P: puttadārassa atthāya hitāya sukhāya hoti; | T: (none)
an5.42:2.5 | P: dāsakammakaraporisassa atthāya hitāya sukhāya hoti; | T: (none)
an5.42:2.6 | P: mittāmaccānaṁ atthāya hitāya sukhāya hoti; | T: (none)
an5.42:2.7 | P: samaṇabrāhmaṇānaṁ atthāya hitāya sukhāya hotīti. | T: ascetics and brahmins.”
an5.42:3.1 | S: The gods protect one who is guarded by principle, | T: The deities protect one guarded by the Dhamma,
an5.42:3.2 | S: who uses their wealth for the welfare of the many. | T: who has managed his wealth for the welfare of many.
an5.42:3.3 | S: One who is learned, <j>with precepts and observances intact, | T: Fame does not forsake one steadfast in the Dhamma, who is learned and of virtuous behavior and observances.
an5.42:3.4 | S: and steady in principle, doesn’t lose their reputation. | T: (none)
an5.42:4.1 | S: Firm in principle, accomplished in ethical conduct, | T: Who is fit to blame him, standing in Dhamma, accomplished in virtuous behavior,
an5.42:4.2 | S: truthful, conscientious; | T: a speaker of truth, possessing a sense of shame,
an5.42:4.3 | S: like a pendant of Black Plum River gold, | T: pure like a coin of refined gold?
an5.42:4.4 | S: who is worthy to criticize them? | T: (none)
an5.42:4.5 | S: Even the gods praise them, | T: Even the devas praise him;
an5.42:4.6 | S: and by the Divinity, too, they’re praised.” | T: by Brahmā, too, he is praised.
an5.42:4.7 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.49
an5.49:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s Park.
an5.49:1.2 | S: Then King Pasenadi of Kosala went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. | T: Then King Pasenadi of Kosala approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, and sat down to one side.
an5.49:2.1 | P: Tena kho pana samayena mallikā devī kālaṅkatā hoti. | T: Now on that occasion Queen Mallikā had just died.
an5.49:2.2 | S: Then a man went up to the king and whispered in his ear, | T: Then a man approached King Pasendi and whispered in his ear:
an5.49:2.3 | S: “Your Majesty, Queen Mallikā has passed away.” | T: “Sire, Queen Mallikā has just died.”
an5.49:2.4 | S: When this was said, King Pasenadi was miserable and sad. He sat with his shoulders drooping, downcast, depressed, with nothing to say. | T: When this was said, King Pasenadi was pained and saddened, and he sat there with slumping shoulders, facing downward, glum, and speechless.
an5.49:3.1 | S: Knowing this, the Buddha said to him, | T: Then the Blessed One, having known the king’s condition, said to him:
an5.49:3.2 | S: “Great king, there are five things that cannot be had by any ascetic or brahmin or god or Māra or divinity or by anyone in the world. | T: “Great king, there are these five situations that are unobtainable by an ascetic or a brahmin, by a deva, Māra, or Brahmā, or by anyone in the world.”
an5.49:3.3 | S: What five? | T: The rest of this sutta is identical with AN5.48, including the verses.
an5.49:3.4 | S: That someone liable to old age should not grow old. … Sorrowing and lamenting doesn’t do even a little bit of good … ‘The deed is powerful. What can I do now?’” | T: (none)
an5.49:3.5 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.39
an8.39:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these eight kinds of overflowing merit, overflowing goodness. They nurture happiness and are conducive to heaven, ripening in happiness and leading to heaven. They lead to what is likable, desirable, agreeable, to welfare and happiness. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these eight streams of merit, streams of the wholesome, nutriments of happiness—heavenly, ripening in happiness, conducive to heaven—that lead to what is wished for, desired, and agreeable, to one’s welfare and happiness.

